  <h1>Dog joint supplements: what works and what's marketing</h1>
  <p class="guide-lede">The saddest sentence in dog ownership is "she just got old." Joints age, but stiffness at seven isn't a life sentence — and the supplement aisle is half genuine help, half expensive powder. Here's how to tell them apart.</p>

  <h2>When should a dog start joint support?</h2>
  <p>Earlier than most people think. Large breeds, working dogs, and breeds prone to hip dysplasia benefit from starting <em>before</em> visible stiffness — prevention beats repair in cartilage, which heals slowly and poorly. If you're already seeing slow stairs, stiff mornings that "warm up," or reluctance to jump into the car, start now.</p>

  <h2>Which ingredients actually have evidence?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Omega-3 fatty acids (EPA/DHA).</strong> The best-supported option — fish-oil omega-3s reduce joint inflammation, and the effect shows up in controlled studies of arthritic dogs.</li>
    <li><strong>Glucosamine + chondroitin.</strong> The classic pairing, building blocks of cartilage. Evidence is mixed but the safety record is long; most vets consider it a reasonable, low-risk bet.</li>
    <li><strong>Green-lipped mussel.</strong> A natural source of omega-3s and glycosaminoglycans with growing supporting research.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Be skeptical of proprietary blends that bury doses ("joint matrix 400mg"), anything promising to "regrow" cartilage, and human supplements repackaged at dog prices — dosing matters and dogs aren't small humans.</p>

  <h2>What matters more than any supplement?</h2>
  <p>Two things, and neither comes in a bottle: <strong>weight</strong> — every extra pound loads the joints, and lean dogs in studies live meaningfully longer with later onset of arthritis — and <strong>consistent moderate exercise</strong>, which keeps the muscle that stabilizes joints. A supplement on top of an overweight, sedentary dog is a rounding error.</p>

  <h2>How long until I see a difference?</h2>
  <p>Joint supplements are slow medicine — expect 4–8 weeks before judging. Watch for the behaviors that faded: jumping into the car, taking stairs at speed, longer walks by choice. If nothing changes by week eight at a proper dose, that supplement isn't the answer for your dog.</p>

  <h2>When is it a vet problem?</h2>
  <p>Limping, yelping, a leg held off the ground, or sudden (rather than gradual) stiffness — those need a diagnosis, not a supplement. And if your dog is on any medication, check with the vet before adding anything.</p>

